WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:08.614 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Mexico's states, part 2 of 2. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.276 --> 00:00:20.364 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We work through 16 of the Mexico regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21.077 --> 00:00:22.946 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Yucatán into view.

4
00:00:33.695 --> 00:00:44.063 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yucatán is the western half of the Yucatán peninsula in the east of the
country, jutting north between the Gulf of Mexico and the Caribbean Sea, with
Mérida in the middle of the state.

5
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:54.182 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name is a Spanish mishearing of the reply the Maya gave when they were
asked, something like I do not understand you, and the story of how it
happened is probably too neat.

6
00:00:54.846 --> 00:01:05.167 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the peninsula of the shadow serpent at Chichén Itzá, where a ring of
cenotes marks the crater of the asteroid that ended the dinosaurs.

7
00:01:05.904 --> 00:01:08.458 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Hidalgo into view.

8
00:01:18.079 --> 00:01:27.379 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Hidalgo lies in the middle of the country, east of Querétaro and north of
Puebla, with Pachuca up on its high eastern plateau.

9
00:01:28.042 --> 00:01:41.219 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Cornish miners worked Real del Monte from 1824 and left behind pasties, still
sold in the town as pastes, and the first football pitch in Mexico.

10
00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:52.527 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the state named for the priest who called for independence, with the
Cornish pasties of the silver mine and the Toltec stone warriors standing on
the pyramid at Tula.

11
00:01:53.228 --> 00:01:55.225 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Veracruz into view.

12
00:02:02.109 --> 00:02:15.925 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Veracruz is the long state that curves along the Gulf of Mexico, running from
Tamaulipas in the north down towards the Isthmus of Tehuantepec in the south,
with the port of Veracruz at the top of the curve.

13
00:02:16.588 --> 00:02:27.467 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Cortés landed on Good Friday in 1519 and named the first Spanish town in
Mexico for the True Cross, and everything Spain took out of Mexico left
through this port for three hundred years.

14
00:02:28.128 --> 00:02:37.973 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the curve of the Gulf coast, the true cross where Cortés landed and
the port that carried Mexico’s silver out to Spain.

15
00:02:38.672 --> 00:02:41.145 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Quintana Roo into view.

16
00:02:48.469 --> 00:02:58.744 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Quintana Roo is the narrow strip of the Caribbean coast in the far north-east,
between Yucatán and the border of Belize, with Cancún on its shore.

17
00:02:59.395 --> 00:03:14.616 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Cancún was an uninhabited sandbar until 1970, when a computer model picked it
as the best site for a resort, and the Mesoamerican reef offshore is the
second-longest barrier reef in the world.

18
00:03:15.273 --> 00:03:25.037 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the resort that grew on a sandbar in a single generation, with the
second-longest reef in the world lying off the beach.

19
00:03:25.758 --> 00:03:27.976 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Tlaxcala into view.

20
00:03:34.231 --> 00:03:45.702 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tlaxcala is the smallest state in Mexico, a small patch in the centre of the
country, ringed by Puebla, the state of México and Hidalgo, with its capital
inside it.

21
00:03:46.364 --> 00:03:55.234 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It allied with Cortés against the Aztecs, which is why its people were never
conquered and were given privileges the rest of Mexico did not receive.

22
00:03:55.893 --> 00:04:04.682 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the smallest state, the one that sided with Cortés and was never
conquered, with the painted battle walls at Cacaxtla.

23
00:04:05.362 --> 00:04:07.405 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Puebla into view.

24
00:04:12.922 --> 00:04:23.023 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Puebla lies in the centre-south of Mexico, west of Veracruz and south of
Tlaxcala, with its capital of the same name in the middle of the state.

25
00:04:23.673 --> 00:04:34.935 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was founded in 1531 as Puebla de los Ángeles, a Spanish town with no
indigenous settlement underneath it, and the name means town of the angels.

26
00:04:35.596 --> 00:04:44.211 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the town of the angels, where a smaller Mexican force won at Cinco de
Mayo and the biggest pyramid by volume has a church on top of it.

27
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.408 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring México into view.

28
00:04:52.395 --> 00:05:01.822 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The state of México lies in the centre of the country, wrapping around the
federal district on three sides, with Toluca in the north-east of it.

29
00:05:02.476 --> 00:05:20.800 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named after the Mexica, the Aztecs’ own name for themselves. Teotihuacán
is here, a city of more than 100,000 people by the year 400, already abandoned
and legendary when the Aztecs found it and gave it its name.

30
00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:29.507 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the state that wraps the capital on three sides, with the huge
pyramid city of Teotihuacán standing out in its fields.

31
00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.381 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Distrito Federal into view.

32
00:05:39.981 --> 00:05:49.699 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Distrito Federal is the federal district in the middle of Mexico, not a
state in the ordinary sense, and the state of México surrounds it on three
sides.

33
00:05:50.360 --> 00:06:08.432 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is built on the drained lake bed of Tenochtitlán and is sinking by up to 50
centimetres a year in places, so the cathedral leans visibly; the map keeps
the old name, though it was officially renamed Ciudad de México in 2016.

34
00:06:09.092 --> 00:06:16.975 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the capital that is not a state, a city sinking into its own old lake
bed with a visibly leaning cathedral.

35
00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:19.785 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Campeche into view.

36
00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:36.639 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Campeche is the western part of the Yucatán peninsula on the Gulf of Mexico,
between Tabasco and Yucatán, with its capital on the coast in the south of the
state.

37
00:06:37.295 --> 00:06:52.481 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its walled city was fortified against pirates in the 1600s and is a UNESCO
site, still ringed by bastions, and the name comes from the Maya words kaan
peech, place of snakes and ticks.

38
00:06:53.133 --> 00:07:01.666 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the walled port that kept the pirates out, and the rival Maya city of
Calakmul whose pyramid rises above the jungle canopy.

39
00:07:02.357 --> 00:07:04.447 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Michoacán into view.

40
00:07:10.058 --> 00:07:19.265 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Michoacán lies on the Pacific in the west of Mexico, with Colima to the west
and Guerrero to the south-east, and Morelia up in its eastern highlands.

41
00:07:19.919 --> 00:07:27.257 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Purépecha people who lived around its lakes were never conquered by the
Aztecs, and their language is related to no other language on earth.

42
00:07:27.922 --> 00:07:37.129 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the unvanquished Purépecha in the west, where tens of millions of
monarch butterflies from Canada and the United States winter on a few hectares
of fir forest.

43
00:07:37.804 --> 00:07:39.801 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Colima into view.

44
00:07:46.172 --> 00:07:55.634 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Colima is a very small state on the Pacific, hemmed in between Jalisco and
Michoacán, with the volcano of the same name rising in the middle of it.

45
00:07:56.288 --> 00:08:09.593 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Volcán de Colima is one of the most active in North America, and the state
administers the Revillagigedo islands, a UNESCO site some 700 kilometres
offshore that is Mexico’s largest marine reserve.

46
00:08:10.251 --> 00:08:18.482 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the little state with the smoking volcano, and the island reserve far
out in the Pacific that belongs to it.

47
00:08:19.169 --> 00:08:21.003 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Morelos into view.

48
00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:36.618 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Morelos is a small state just south of the federal district, between the state
of México and Guerrero, with Cuernavaca in the middle of it.

49
00:08:37.271 --> 00:08:48.277 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Cuernavaca’s mild weather made it the retreat of the Aztec emperors, then of
Cortés, then of Maximilian, and it is called the city of eternal spring.

50
00:08:48.938 --> 00:08:55.927 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the city of eternal spring, where emperors and generals went to
retire and where Zapata was born.

51
00:08:56.704 --> 00:08:59.049 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Tabasco into view.

52
00:09:13.493 --> 00:09:24.070 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tabasco is the narrow coastal plain on the Gulf of Mexico between Veracruz and
Chiapas, with Villahermosa on the Grijalva river in the middle of the state.

53
00:09:24.733 --> 00:09:37.992 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Olmecs, the first Mesoamerican civilisation, were here three thousand
years ago and carved the colossal basalt heads, and about a third of all the
water in Mexico’s rivers runs through this state.

54
00:09:38.659 --> 00:09:44.963 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the wettest state, where the sauce comes from and the Olmec stone
heads stand in the jungle.

55
00:09:45.694 --> 00:09:47.946 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Guerrero into view.

56
00:09:55.550 --> 00:10:05.395 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Guerrero lies on the Pacific in the south-west of Mexico, south of Michoacán
and west of Oaxaca, with Acapulco on the coast.

57
00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:15.267 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Acapulco was the Pacific end of the Manila galleon route for 250 years,
carrying Asian silk and porcelain to Spain across two oceans.

58
00:10:15.927 --> 00:10:23.172 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the Pacific end of the galleons, and the white silver town of Taxco
climbing the steep hill inland.

59
00:10:23.868 --> 00:10:27.107 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Oaxaca into view.

60
00:10:33.273 --> 00:10:45.766 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Oaxaca lies in the south of Mexico, with the long Sierra Madre and the Mixteca
across its middle and a short stretch of Pacific coast in the south-west, and
its capital of the same name near the centre.

61
00:10:46.416 --> 00:10:56.993 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is the most linguistically diverse state in Mexico, with sixteen indigenous
language groups, and many of its towns still govern by customary law rather
than party politics.

62
00:10:57.651 --> 00:11:10.144 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the state of sixteen languages and village councils, with Monte Albán
above the city and the mole and mezcal the world pictures.

63
00:11:10.826 --> 00:11:12.951 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Chiapas into view.

64
00:11:17.912 --> 00:11:27.548 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Chiapas is the southernmost state of Mexico, touching the Pacific in the south
and the Gulf of Mexico in the north, with Tuxtla Gutiérrez in the middle of
the highlands.

65
00:11:28.211 --> 00:11:40.193 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Palenque here holds the tomb of Pakal, whose carved jade sarcophagus lid is
among the most famous objects in the Maya world, and the state grows more of
Mexico’s coffee than any other.

66
00:11:40.853 --> 00:11:52.579 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the state where the Zapatista uprising began on New Year’s Day in
1994, with the coffee on the slopes and the Maya tomb at Palenque.

67
00:11:58.664 --> 00:12:24.980 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
16 regions of Mexico, one for each target of the map: Yucatán, Hidalgo,
Veracruz, Quintana Roo, Tlaxcala, Puebla, México, Distrito Federal, Campeche,
Michoacán, Colima, Morelos, Tabasco, Guerrero, Oaxaca and Chiapas.

68
00:12:25.645 --> 00:12:34.689 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the shape of Mexico from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
